---
import { readJson } from "../../lib/data";
const { pagan } = readJson("src/content/docs/meta.json");
import Landing from "../../layouts/Landing.astro";
---

<Landing
  title="Pixel art MCP server · PAGAN"
  description="PAGAN's MCP server gives any AI agent tools to write, lint, preview and build game art: sprites, tiles, 3D-rendered headings, animations. Free, open source, local."
  heading="A pixel art MCP server that checks its own work"
  points={[
    "Tools: <code>write_asset</code> (writes, lints and returns a preview image), <code>preview</code>, <code>lint</code>, <code>fit</code>, <code>palette_adjust</code>, <code>build</code>, <code>manifest</code> and more.",
    "Previews come back as images, so the model looks at what it made and fixes it.",
    "Resources: the full workflow and notation reference, the project's brief, style guide and manifest.",
    "The same JSON as the CLI's <code>--json</code>: one contract for agents and scripts.",
    "Works with Claude Desktop, Claude Code, Cursor or your own agent. Runs locally over stdio; no cloud, no key. Listed in the MCP Registry as io.github.lukasgolino/pagan.",
  ]}
  code={`claude mcp add pagan -- uvx pagan-gen@${pagan} mcp --project /path/to/art

# any other client: command "uvx", args ["pagan-gen@${pagan}", "mcp", "--project", "/path/to/art"]
# or pip install pagan-gen==${pagan}, then command "pagan", args ["mcp", "--project", "/path/to/art"]`}
>
  <p>
    Most pixel-art MCP servers let a model set pixels one call at a time. PAGAN's tools work on source
    files instead: the agent writes a sprite grid or a 3D voxel model, PAGAN lints it and renders it, and
    every heading and frame comes out consistent.
  </p>
</Landing>
